      "text": "I can’t honestly give genome-personalized training/diet recommendations from this turn, because the live GenomeClaw service is not healthy right now: the health, findings, and PRS endpoints all returned HTTP 503, and the gene-panel calls did not reach the host cleanly. So I don’t have your actual ACTN3/ACE/FTO/APOE/TCF7L2/etc. data to personalize from.\n\nWhat I would query once the service is back:\n\n- **Muscle/training panel**: **ACTN3** fast-twitch/power signal, **ACE** endurance–power tendency, **AMPD1** exercise intolerance risk, **MSTN/IGF1/VDR** muscle and adaptation biology, **COL5A1/COL1A1** tendon/injury context.\n- **Diet/metabolic panel**: **FTO/MC4R** appetite/body-weight tendency, **APOE** lipid response, **TCF7L2/PPARG/SLC30A8** glycemic risk, **LCT** lactose tolerance, **CYP1A2/ADORA2A** caffeine response, **ALDH2/ADH1B** alcohol metabolism, **FADS1/FADS2** fatty-acid metabolism.\n- **PRS audit**: cardiometabolic PRSs such as CAD/lipid and type-2-diabetes risk if already computed; I would be cautious about “muscle-building PRSs” because training-response PRSs are generally less mature and often not individually actionable.\n\nUntil I can see your genome, the best **non-personalized baseline** for general fitness muscle gain is:\n\n1. **Lift 3–4 days/week**, emphasizing progressive overload on squat/hinge/push/pull/carry patterns.  \n2. Aim for roughly **10–20 hard sets per muscle group per week**, adjusted by recovery.  \n3. Use mostly **6–12 rep sets**, with some heavier 3–6 rep work and some higher-rep accessory work.  \n4. Eat **~1.6–2.2 g/kg/day protein**, spread over 3–5 meals; this range is supported by meta-analysis and sports-nutrition consensus.  \n5. For diet, default to: sufficient calories for your goal, high fiber, minimally processed carbs, unsaturated fats, enough protein, and track body weight/performance for 2–4 weeks before changing anything.  \n6. Use caffeine, alcohol, lactose, and high-saturated-fat choices based 